@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

#
# prima-materia — the clause that puts the effect beyond the bystander
#
# One generalization, formed from three attested claims in three transmissions
# between which no route has been proposed: the Pāli commentarial handbook of
# the fifth century, the Chinese inner-chapters of the fourth, and the Latin
# ceremonial magic of the sixteenth.
#
# It is the near neighbour of pmc:TheUnsympatheticWitnessAsStatedCauseOfFailure
# and differs from it at the point that decides what a protocol can do with
# either. That statement is about an account given for a session that produced
# nothing, and such an account is offered after the fact. This one is about a
# clause that stands in the operative instruction, before any session, as a
# property of the operation. The first can be read as evasion; the second
# cannot, because there is nothing yet to evade.
#
# The counter-search required by SPEC §16 was carried over the compiled holding
# before this was written and returned one case that bears on the statement.
# It is named in the notes and it is the reason for the second clause.
#

pmc:TheEffectPutBeyondTheBystander a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "The clause that puts the effect beyond the bystander"@en, "Die Klausel, die die Wirkung dem Zuschauer entzieht"@de ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where a transmission teaches an operation whose effect, if it occurred, would be evident to anyone standing by, the operative passage itself carries a clause that puts the effect beyond a bystander's reach: by reserving the perception of it to the operator, by reserving its occasion to a case that may not arise in an ordinary life, or by making the effect depend on the operation being kept from the public. The clause stands as a property of the operation and not as an account of a session that produced nothing. Where instead the outcome a transmission stakes itself on is one a stranger can read off independently of the operator, no such clause appears and the operation is shown." ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:TassaYevaSaPathavi, pmc:GehongFangshuWuyi, pmc:AgrippaSilentium ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    skos:note "The three witnesses, each with its own device. The Pāli handbook, instructing how to turn ground into water and water into ground, adds that only for him does the ground become water and only for him the water earth, that for everybody else it stays what it was and people go on ploughing and the fishes go on swimming, and that if he wishes it may be water for them too. The Chinese chapter, having stated a limit against its own side — that the meditative arts cannot lengthen a life or dispel an illness — reserves their worth to the violent ghost and the great epidemic, and concedes that where those do not arise, having the arts and not having them come to exactly the same. The Latin book, at the head of its third part, says that every magical experiment flees the public, seeks to be hid and is strengthened by silence, but is destroyed by publication, neither does any complete effect follow after."@en ;
    skos:note "What makes the three one claim is not that they conceal. It is where the concealment is put. None of these is a rule of secrecy addressed to the practitioner about what he may tell; all three are statements about the operation, standing in the passage that prescribes it, and each of them has the consequence that a person who watched the operation and saw nothing has seen exactly what the text predicted he would see. That is a property of the claim and not of the practitioner, and it can be established by reading."@en ;
    skos:note "The first witness is the sharpest and the reason the statement was written. The operation it belongs to — a road becoming water, a river becoming ground — is the most public event anything in this holding claims; and the same paragraph that prescribes it withdraws it from every eye but one, while leaving the extension to other eyes at the operator's wish, which is itself unobservable. The handbook is not defending the claim. It has no opponent in view at that place and is giving instructions."@en ;
    skos:note "The counter-search, and the case it returned. The holding carries a transmission that did put its operation before doubters as its normal business: an American mental operator of the eighteen-forties who travelled for years giving exhibitions to rooms that held the thing for a fraud and threatened him with mob violence, at pmp:QuimbyExhibitioPublica. It is not a counterexample, and the second clause of the statement is where it lands. What he exhibited was a subject describing a place someone in the audience was thinking of, or the inside of a body a physician could afterwards examine — outcomes a stranger reads off without the operator. The same volume records that he had earlier held the standard excluding condition, that a sceptic sitting too near made him fail, and dropped it in the same movement by which his outcomes became checkable."@en ;
    skos:note "Its relation to pmc:TheUnsympatheticWitnessAsStatedCauseOfFailure and to pmc:TheRivalWhoWasNotInTheRoom. All three turn on whether the thing claimed can be established by somebody other than the parties to it, and they are three answers to the same pressure at three different places in a transmission: a clause in the instruction, a named cause of failure, and an account of a session. The order matters for a protocol. A transmission that carries the clause has nothing to explain afterwards, so the absence of the other two at such a transmission is not evidence that it is more candid."@en ;
    skos:note "What it does not say. It says nothing about whether any of these operations produces anything, and nothing about the motives of anyone who wrote the clauses. It is a statement about the shape a transmission takes, it can be settled by reading, and that is why it stands here and not at a protocol."@en ;
    skos:note "Falsified by a transmission that teaches an operation of this kind — an effect on the material world that any bystander would see if it occurred — and whose operative passage states that bystanders do see it, or that their not seeing it counts against the operation. Sharper still, and the shape to look for first: an operative text that prescribes the operation be performed before a stranger as a proof. The corpora likeliest to carry it are the ones in this holding where a feat is performed before an assembly as part of the office rather than as a display: the Siberian, Chukchee and Inuit material has not been asked this question, and the recorded duty of the great shaman to exercise his art before a gathering in order to convince people is the first place to put it."@en ;
    skos:note "The weakness of the derivation, stated because a reader would otherwise have to find it. The three devices are not the same device, and a critic may fairly say that a reservation to the operator, a reservation to a rare occasion and a destruction by publicity have only their consequence in common. That is the claim: they have their consequence in common, and the consequence is the thing a protocol meets. But a fourth witness using a fourth device would strengthen it less than a second witness using the first device would, and the first device is the one the holding has only once."@en ;
    skos:note "No pm:Testing points at this node, and that is its position in the queue and not a defect."@en .
